1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:02,350
The Curiosity Club.

2
00:00:02,350 --> 00:00:04,714
Episode 37.

3
00:00:04,714 --> 00:00:07,391
The Bicycle with the Giant Wheel.

4
00:00:07,391 --> 00:00:10,108
Here is something to listen for.

5
00:00:10,108 --> 00:00:14,850
Why did the club remove its large applause machine from the bicycle design?

6
00:00:14,850 --> 00:00:16,277
Ready?

7
00:00:16,277 --> 00:00:18,355
Let us find out.

8
00:00:18,355 --> 00:00:25,502
At a transport museum, Leo stops beside a bicycle with an enormous front wheel and a tiny back wheel.

9
00:00:25,502 --> 00:00:29,510
The wheels forgot to agree on a size, he says.

10
00:00:29,510 --> 00:00:32,010
Pip studies the high seat.

11
00:00:32,010 --> 00:00:35,788
I would need a ladder and a very encouraging friend.

12
00:00:35,788 --> 00:00:43,152
The museum educator explains that on many high-wheel bicycles, the pedals directly turned the large front wheel.

13
00:00:43,152 --> 00:00:50,285
A larger wheel traveled farther with each turn, but the high riding position brought difficulties and risks.

14
00:00:50,285 --> 00:00:54,646
Maya compares it with a later bicycle in the next display.

15
00:00:54,646 --> 00:00:59,103
That one has two more similar-sized wheels and a chain drive.

16
00:00:59,103 --> 00:01:04,605
The chain carries motion from the pedals to the rear wheel, the educator says.

17
00:01:04,605 --> 00:01:08,994
This arrangement helped designers use a lower riding position.

18
00:01:08,994 --> 00:01:13,491
So the old bicycle was not simply silly, Maya says.

19
00:01:13,491 --> 00:01:17,092
It was part of people trying different solutions.

20
00:01:17,092 --> 00:01:18,899
Exactly.

21
00:01:18,899 --> 00:01:24,116
An invention has a history of ideas, trade-offs, and improvements.

22
00:01:24,116 --> 00:01:27,921
The club sketches an imaginary future bicycle.

23
00:01:27,921 --> 00:01:30,353
Leo adds a rain cover.

24
00:01:30,353 --> 00:01:35,122
Maya adds a place for a schoolbag that will not interfere with riding.

25
00:01:35,122 --> 00:01:39,524
Pip adds automatic applause whenever the rider climbs a hill.

26
00:01:39,524 --> 00:01:42,092
How heavy would all that make it?

27
00:01:42,092 --> 00:01:44,538
the educator asks.

28
00:01:44,538 --> 00:01:46,372
They pause.

29
00:01:46,372 --> 00:01:49,388
Every extra feature has a cost.

30
00:01:49,388 --> 00:01:54,483
Pip removes the large applause machine and replaces it with one small bell.

31
00:01:54,483 --> 00:01:57,744
A lighter celebration, he says.

32
00:01:57,744 --> 00:02:02,771
Maya writes their design question: Better for whom, and better at what?

33
00:02:02,771 --> 00:02:05,679
Now, a little more to discover.

34
00:02:05,679 --> 00:02:11,195
Many historic high-wheel bicycles connected the pedals directly to the front wheel.

35
00:02:11,195 --> 00:02:17,540
Later chain-driven designs helped make lower bicycles with more similar wheel sizes practical.

36
00:02:17,540 --> 00:02:24,456
Design changes involve trade-offs among speed, comfort, control, weight, and other goals.

37
00:02:24,456 --> 00:02:28,967
Newer does not explain which particular feature solves a problem.

38
00:02:28,967 --> 00:02:31,807
Let us hear the main idea again.

39
00:02:31,807 --> 00:02:35,638
The giant wheel reflected an earlier design approach.

40
00:02:35,638 --> 00:02:41,630
Comparing it with a chain-driven bicycle showed how mechanisms and riding position changed.

41
00:02:41,630 --> 00:02:47,336
The club then discovered that adding features to its own design also added trade-offs.

42
00:02:47,336 --> 00:02:50,312
Here are three useful expressions.

43
00:02:50,312 --> 00:02:53,437
You can listen, or say them with me.

44
00:02:53,437 --> 00:02:55,815
How did this design work?

45
00:02:55,815 --> 00:02:58,192
How did this design work?

46
00:02:58,192 --> 00:03:00,964
What problem did the change solve?

47
00:03:00,964 --> 00:03:03,736
What problem did the change solve?

48
00:03:03,736 --> 00:03:06,847
It is useful, but it adds weight.

49
00:03:06,847 --> 00:03:09,959
It is useful, but it adds weight.

50
00:03:09,959 --> 00:03:12,554
Remember our listening question?

51
00:03:12,554 --> 00:03:17,296
Why did the club remove its large applause machine from the bicycle design?

52
00:03:17,296 --> 00:03:19,429
Here is the answer.

53
00:03:19,429 --> 00:03:24,646
It added too much weight for a small benefit, so they chose a lighter feature.

54
00:03:24,646 --> 00:03:28,301
And here is a question just for your imagination.

55
00:03:28,301 --> 00:03:33,111
What one improvement would make an everyday object easier for you to use?

56
00:03:33,111 --> 00:03:36,222
There is more than one possible answer.

57
00:03:36,222 --> 00:03:39,470
Keep wondering, and see you next time.
